mode: "100644"
text: "# Examples — carousels slide by slide\n\nWorked carousels showing the spine, the slide text, and the design/image direction — plus a\ntext-dump version for contrast. Voice is the blunt indie-founder voice from earlier skills (short,\ndry, no exclamation marks, concrete). Your output uses the loaded `voice.md`.\n\n---\n\n## Example 1 — How-to carousel (Instagram, 4:5) · goal: saves\n\n**Spine:** cover promises 5 lessons from deleting a feature → one lesson per slide → payoff → save.\n\n- **Slide 1 (cover):** \"We deleted our most-requested feature. 5 lessons so you don't waste 6 months →\"\n- **Slide 2:** \"1. 'Requested' isn't 'used.' / Watch behavior, not surveys.\"\n- **Slide 3:** \"2. The boring thing wins. / Our dull export button beat the big feature.\"\n- **Slide 4:** \"3. Sales calls aren't a roadmap. / Prospects describe what closes *them*.\"\n- **Slide 5:** \"4. Every feature is forever. / Docs, bugs, support, clutter. Deleting helped.\"\n- **Slide 6:** \"5. We built to feel productive, not to be useful. / Now: behavior first.\"\n- **Slide 7 (payoff):** \"Build less. Watch more. Ship the boring thing.\"\n- **Slide 8 (CTA):** \"Save this for your next roadmap meeting. / @handle\"\n\n**Design direction:** 4:5, off-white background, one ink headline + one gray supporting line, a\nsingle green accent on the number, \"n/8\" bottom-left, identical layout every slide.\n\n**Cover image prompt (Ideogram/Nano Banana):**\n> \"A portrait 4:5 social carousel cover slide. Minimal editorial style, off-white background, large\n> bold dark headline, one small green accent. Headline text (render exactly): 'We deleted our\n> most-requested feature.' Supporting line: '5 lessons so you don't waste 6 months →'. High\n> contrast, generous margins, mobile-legible, no clutter.\"\n\n*(Body slides reuse the same skeleton, swapping the headline/line and number.)*\n\n---\n\n## Example 2 — Myth-bust carousel (LinkedIn PDF) · goal: authority + saves\n\n- **Cover:** \"4 things you believe about LinkedIn that are killing your reach →\"\n- **Slide 2:** \"Myth: post every day. / Reality: post when you have something. Quality drives dwell.\"\n- **Slide 3:** \"Myth: links get clicks. / Reality: links in the post suppress reach. Put them in\n  comments.\"\n- **Slide 4:** \"Myth: more hashtags = more reach. / Reality: 3–5 relevant ones. The rest is noise.\"\n- **Slide 5:** \"Myth: company pages win. / Reality: people follow people. Post from a person.\"\n- **Slide 6 (payoff):** \"Reach isn't a volume game. It's a dwell-and-comments game.\"\n- **Slide 7 (CTA):** \"Save this. Then go fix your next post. / @handle\"\n\n**Design:** square PDF, clean professional template, consistent header band, slide number, brand\naccent. (Post copy + the link-in-comments handled by `linkedin-post-writer`.)\n\n---\n\n## Contrast — the text-dump version (do NOT do this)\n\n- A cover that's a small-type paragraph with no hook.\n- Slides each packed with 4–5 sentences, edge to edge, different layouts.\n- No spine, no momentum, no save-CTA.\n\n→ Nobody swipes past slide 1, and nobody saves it. One idea per slide, big legible type, a cover\nthat promises the swipe — that's the difference.\n\n---\n\n## What the good examples share\n\n- A **cover that hooks + promises** the swipe, legible at thumbnail.\n- **One idea per slide**, headline + ≤1 line, consistent layout.\n- A clear **spine** and **momentum** to the last slide.\n- A **payoff + one save-leaning CTA**.\n- A **consistent design direction** + ready image prompts (or a template spec).\n- On-voice, no fabricated stats, AI-disclosure where generated.\n"
